<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-skew-lines-and-projection-of-a-line" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Skew Lines and Projection of a Line onto a Plane</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.0 Measurements and Geometry
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.4 Three Dimensional Geometry
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Identify skew lines and projection of a line onto a plane
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        12
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- Planning notes (from the Combined Mathematics tab of the Grade 12 Book Structure Mapping sheet)
         Content summary: Skew lines are lines in space that are neither parallel nor intersecting. The projection of a line onto a plane is the line joining the feet of the perpendiculars dropped from its end points to the plane.
         Suggested activity: Using a cuboid made from sticks and plasticine (or the edges of the classroom), learners identify pairs of skew edges, then hang a string with a stone from a point on a slanting stick to the floor to show the projection of the stick onto the floor.
         Interactive (JSXGraph): Rotatable 3D cuboid (JSXGraph view3d) with a draggable line from a top vertex; its projection onto the base is drawn as a dashed line, and two skew edges can be highlighted. -->

    <!-- Section notes (2026-11)
         SLOs: Core 2.4 (a): parallel, intersecting and skew lines; projection of a point and of a line onto a plane
         Activity: as planning notes (a stick-and-plasticine cuboid, or the classroom's edges; a plumb line shows the projection)
         JSXGraph: fig-skew-lines-and-projection (assets/jsxgraph/skew-lines-and-projection.js) | changed: sliders p and q place P on the base instead of a draggable line (a JSXGraph 1.8.0 3D point drags only within a plane); a pair slider highlights skew, parallel and intersecting edges
         Key inquiry question: kiq-three-dimensional-geometry, placed in the section body (an introduction is not allowed in a section without subsections)
         STACK: assets/stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/09-three-dimensional-geometry/01-skew-lines-and-projection-of-a-line/ -->

    <question xml:id="kiq-three-dimensional-geometry">
        <p><em>Where do we use the lengths and angles of solid shapes in everyday life?</em></p>
    </question>

    <p>
        In Grade 10 you used sine, cosine and tangent to find sides and angles of right-angled triangles, including angles of elevation, and you found the surface area and volume of prisms and pyramids, where the slant height of a pyramid is not the same as its height. In Grade 11 you used the sine rule and the cosine rule in triangles with no right angle. This chapter puts these tools to work inside solid shapes.
    </p>
    <p>
        A fundi in Nyeri cutting rafters for a hip roof needs the length of each sloping edge and the slope of each face before the first piece of timber is cut. A tent maker needs the length of a guy rope from the top of a pole to a peg. In each case the line we want lies inside a solid. The method of this chapter is always the same: find a triangle that contains the line, then flatten it and use the trigonometry you already know.
    </p>

    <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
        <ol>
            <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> two lines in space are parallel, intersecting or skew; skew lines lie in no common plane; a line perpendicular to a plane; the projection of a point and of a line onto a plane.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> classify pairs of edges and diagonals of cuboids, prisms and pyramids, and name the projection of a line on a face or on the base.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> edges of a classroom, a plumb line and a flagpole wire.</p></li>
        </ol>
    </p>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
            <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners find pairs of edges that neither meet nor are parallel, and see a projection as the <q>shadow</q> found with a plumb line.</p></li>
            <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Put learners in groups of four. Each group builds a cuboid <m>30\,\text{cm}</m> by <m>20\,\text{cm}</m> by <m>15\,\text{cm}</m> from twelve sticks (or straws) joined with plasticine, and labels the corners <m>ABCDEFGH</m> with paper flags: <m>ABCD</m> on the table and <m>E</m>, <m>F</m>, <m>G</m>, <m>H</m> above <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, <m>C</m>, <m>D</m>. Groups without sticks use the edges of the classroom for parts (a) and (b). After part (b), ask: <q>Can you lay a flat sheet of card so that it touches both of these edges along their whole length?</q></p></li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="act-edges-in-the-classroom">
        <title>Edges in the Classroom</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Look at the edges of your classroom, where two walls meet, or a wall meets the floor or the ceiling. Then use your stick model of the cuboid <m>ABCDEFGH</m>.</p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>Name two edges of the classroom that are parallel, and two that meet.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>For example, the four vertical corners of the room are parallel to each other; the edge where the front wall meets the floor meets each vertical corner of the front wall.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Look at the edge where the front wall meets the ceiling and the edge where the left-hand wall meets the floor. Do they meet? Are they parallel?</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Neither. They never meet, and they point in different directions. No flat sheet can touch both along their whole length: they are skew.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>On your model, list every edge that neither meets <m>AB</m> nor is parallel to it.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>CG</m>, <m>DH</m>, <m>FG</m> and <m>HE</m>: four edges. The other edges either meet <m>AB</m> (at <m>A</m> or <m>B</m>) or are parallel to it (<m>DC</m>, <m>EF</m>, <m>HG</m>).</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Push a straw through the model from <m>A</m> to <m>G</m>. Hang a small stone on a thread from <m>G</m>. Where does the thread touch the base? Which line on the base lies directly below <m>AG</m>?</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>The thread touches the base at <m>C</m>, so the line on the base directly below <m>AG</m> is <m>AC</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Hang the thread from the midpoint of <m>AG</m>. Where does it touch the base?</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>At the midpoint of <m>AC</m>. Every point of <m>AG</m> lies directly above a point of <m>AC</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Hang the thread from <m>G</m> again and look at the edge <m>CG</m>. What lies directly below the whole edge?</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Only the single point <m>C</m>: the edge is vertical.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>To formalise parallel, intersecting and skew lines, and the projection of a line onto a plane.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> part (b) and part (c) found skew lines; part (d) and part (e) found a projection with the plumb line.</p></li>
                <li><p>Two lines can sit in three ways: parallel, intersecting or skew. Skew lines are only possible in three dimensions.</p></li>
                <li><p>The projection of a line joins the feet of the perpendiculars from its ends. A vertical line projects onto a horizontal plane as a single point.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Real-world link:</alert> at noon near the equator the sun is almost straight overhead, so the shadow of a leaning pole on level ground is close to its projection on the ground.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <insight xml:id="insight-lines-in-space">
        <title>Lines in Space</title>
        <p>
            Two straight lines in space are
            <ul>
                <li><p><term>parallel</term> if they point in the same direction and never meet;</p></li>
                <li><p><term>intersecting</term> if they meet at one point;</p></li>
                <li><p><term>skew</term> if they never meet and are not parallel.</p></li>
            </ul>
            Parallel lines lie in one plane, and so do intersecting lines. Skew lines never meet and are not parallel: no flat surface holds both. <q>Line <m>AB</m></q> means the whole straight line through <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, not only the segment between them.
        </p>
    </insight>

    <insight xml:id="insight-projection-of-a-line">
        <title>Projection of a Line onto a Plane</title>
        <p>
            A line is <term>perpendicular to a plane</term> when it is at right angles to every line in the plane that passes through its <term>foot</term>, the point where it meets the plane. A vertical pole on level ground and a plumb line are perpendicular to the ground.
        </p>
        <p>
            The <term>projection</term> of a point on a plane is the foot of the perpendicular from the point to the plane. The projection of a line segment on a plane joins the projections of its ends. It is where the line's shadow falls when the light is straight above the plane. A segment that lies in the plane is its own projection; a segment perpendicular to the plane projects to a single point.
        </p>
    </insight>

    <p>
        In this chapter a cuboid is always lettered <m>ABCDEFGH</m>: the base is <m>ABCD</m>, going round, and <m>E</m>, <m>F</m>, <m>G</m>, <m>H</m> are directly above <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, <m>C</m>, <m>D</m>. <m>AB</m> is the length, <m>BC</m> the width and <m>AE</m> the height. In the drawings the face <m>ABFE</m> is at the front and the corner <m>D</m> is hidden, so the edges <m>DA</m>, <m>DC</m> and <m>DH</m> are dashed. A cube is a cuboid whose edges are all equal.
    </p>
    <p>
        A pyramid <m>VABCD</m> has its base <m>ABCD</m> lettered like the base of a cuboid, and <m>O</m> is the centre of the base, where its diagonals cross. It is a <term>right pyramid</term> when its vertex <m>V</m> is directly above <m>O</m>; then <m>VO</m> is its height.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-lines-in-a-cuboid">
