    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example xml:id="example-parallel-intersecting-or-skew">
        <title>Parallel, Intersecting or Skew</title>
        <statement>
            <p>In the cuboid <m>ABCDEFGH</m> of <xref ref="fig-lines-in-a-cuboid"/>, decide whether each pair of lines is parallel, intersecting or skew.
                (a) <m>AB</m> and <m>HG</m> (b) <m>AG</m> and <m>CE</m> (c) <m>BC</m> and <m>AE</m> (d) <m>AF</m> and <m>DG</m> (e) <m>AF</m> and <m>CH</m></p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>(a) <m>HG</m> is the top edge directly above and behind <m>AB</m>, pointing the same way: parallel.</p>
            <p>(b) <m>A</m>, <m>C</m>, <m>G</m> and <m>E</m> are the corners of the rectangle <m>ACGE</m>, which stands upright inside the cuboid. <m>AG</m> and <m>CE</m> are its diagonals, so they cross at its centre, which is the centre of the cuboid: intersecting.</p>
            <p>(c) <m>AE</m> is the vertical edge through <m>A</m>. <m>BC</m> lies in the base and does not pass through <m>A</m>, so the lines never meet; one is vertical and the other horizontal, so they are not parallel: skew.</p>
            <p>(d) <m>AF</m> and <m>DG</m> are diagonals of the opposite faces <m>ABFE</m> and <m>DCGH</m>, both rising from left to right in the same way: parallel.</p>
            <p>(e) <m>AF</m> and <m>CH</m> lie in the parallel faces <m>ABFE</m> and <m>DCGH</m>, so they never meet, but they slope in opposite ways, so they are not parallel: skew.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-skew-edges-of-a-pyramid">
        <title>Skew Edges of a Pyramid</title>
        <statement>
            <p><m>VABCD</m> is a right pyramid on a square base <m>ABCD</m>, so its vertex <m>V</m> is directly above the centre <m>O</m> of the base. Which edges of the pyramid are skew to <m>VA</m>? Is the base diagonal <m>BD</m> skew to <m>VA</m>?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>The edges <m>VB</m>, <m>VC</m> and <m>VD</m> meet <m>VA</m> at <m>V</m>, and the base edges <m>AB</m> and <m>AD</m> meet it at <m>A</m>. The remaining edges, <m>BC</m> and <m>CD</m>, never meet <m>VA</m> and are not parallel to it: they are skew to <m>VA</m>.</p>
            <p><m>VA</m> lies in the upright plane <m>VAC</m>. <m>BD</m> crosses that plane only at <m>O</m>, the centre of the base, and <m>O</m> is not on <m>VA</m>. So <m>BD</m> never meets <m>VA</m>, and it is not parallel to it: <m>BD</m> is skew to <m>VA</m>.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-naming-projections">
        <title>Naming Projections</title>
        <statement>
            <p>(a) In the cuboid <m>ABCDEFGH</m>, name the projection of
                (i) <m>AG</m> on the base <m>ABCD</m>; (ii) <m>AG</m> on the face <m>BCGF</m>; (iii) <m>HB</m> on the base; (iv) <m>EC</m> on the face <m>ABFE</m>.</p>
            <p>(b) A tent at a scouts' camp near Naro Moru is a triangular prism <m>ABCDEF</m>, shown in <xref ref="fig-tent-prism"/>. Its ends <m>ABC</m> and <m>DEF</m> are triangles with <m>CA = CB</m>, it rests on the rectangle <m>ABED</m>, and <m>CF</m> is the ridge. <m>N</m> and <m>P</m> are the midpoints of <m>AB</m> and <m>DE</m>. Name the projection of <m>AF</m> on the ground.</p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-tent-prism">
                <caption>The tent <m>ABCDEF</m>, with <m>N</m> and <m>P</m> the midpoints of <m>AB</m> and <m>DE</m>.</caption>
                <image width="60%">
                    <shortdescription>A tent shaped as a triangular prism ABCDEF: triangular ends ABC and DEF, ridge CF, ground ABED. N and P are the midpoints of AB and DE; CN and FP are dotted vertical lines. AB is 2.4 m, CA = CB = 1.5 m and AD is 3 m. The line AF is drawn thick and its projection AP on the ground is drawn thick dashed.</shortdescription>
                    <latex-image>
\tdplotsetmaincoords{70}{120}
\begin{tikzpicture}[tdplot_main_coords, scale=1.9]
\coordinate (A) at (2.4,3,0);
\coordinate (B) at (0,3,0);
\coordinate (C) at (1.2,3,0.9);
\coordinate (D) at (2.4,0,0);
\coordinate (E) at (0,0,0);
\coordinate (F) at (1.2,0,0.9);
\coordinate (N) at (1.2,3,0);
\coordinate (P) at (1.2,0,0);
\draw[dashed] (D) -- (E) -- (B); \draw[dashed] (E) -- (F);
\draw (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- cycle; \draw (A) -- (D) -- (F) -- (C);
\node[anchor=north] at (A) {$A$};
\node[anchor=west] at (B) {$B$};
\node[anchor=south west] at (C) {$C$};
\node[anchor=north east] at (D) {$D$};
\node[anchor=south] at (E) {$E$};
\node[anchor=south] at (F) {$F$};
\node[anchor=north west] at (N) {$N$};
\node[anchor=north west] at (P) {$P$};
\draw[dotted, thick] (C) -- (N); \draw[dotted, thick] (F) -- (P); \fill (N) circle (1.2pt); \fill (P) circle (1.2pt);
\draw[very thick] (A) -- (F); \draw[very thick, dashed] (A) -- (P);
\draw (1.200,3.000,0.150) -- (1.350,3.000,0.150) -- (1.350,3.000,0.000);
\node[anchor=north] at (2.4,1.5,0) {$3$ m}; \node[anchor=north west] at (0.6,3,0) {$2.4$ m}; \node[anchor=west] at (0.6,3,0.45) {$1.5$ m};
\end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>For each line, drop a perpendicular from each end to the plane and join the feet.</p>
            <p>(a) (i) <m>A</m> is in the base and <m>G</m> is above <m>C</m>: the projection is <m>AC</m>. (ii) <m>G</m> is in the face <m>BCGF</m>, and the perpendicular from <m>A</m> to that face is the edge <m>AB</m>, so <m>A</m> projects to <m>B</m>: the projection is <m>BG</m>. (iii) <m>H</m> is above <m>D</m> and <m>B</m> is in the base: <m>DB</m>. (iv) <m>E</m> is in the face <m>ABFE</m> and the perpendicular from <m>C</m> to that face is the edge <m>CB</m>: <m>EB</m>.</p>
            <p>(b) <m>A</m> is on the ground. <m>F</m> is the top of the back end; the end <m>DEF</m> is isosceles, so <m>F</m> is directly above <m>P</m>, the midpoint of <m>DE</m>. The projection of <m>AF</m> on the ground is <m>AP</m>.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

    <p>Choose from each list. Type decimals with a point, for example <c>8.94</c>. A length may also be typed exactly, for example <c>sqrt(194)</c>.</p>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-parallel-intersecting-or-skew-lines">
        <!--<title></title>-->
        <stack label="stk-parallel-intersecting-or-skew-lines" source="stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/09-three-dimensional-geometry/01-skew-lines-and-projection-of-a-line/Parallel-intersecting-or-skew-lines.xml"/>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-projection-of-a-line-onto-a-plane">
        <!--<title></title>-->
        <stack label="stk-projection-of-a-line-onto-a-plane" source="stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/09-three-dimensional-geometry/01-skew-lines-and-projection-of-a-line/Projection-of-a-line-onto-a-plane.xml"/>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-an-edge-skew-to-a-given-line">
        <!--<title></title>-->
        <stack label="stk-an-edge-skew-to-a-given-line" source="stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/09-three-dimensional-geometry/01-skew-lines-and-projection-of-a-line/An-edge-skew-to-a-given-line.xml"/>
    </exercise>

    <exercises xml:id="exercises-skew-lines-and-projection">
        <exercise xml:id="ex-classify-lines-in-a-cuboid">
            <statement><p>In the cuboid <m>ABCDEFGH</m>, decide whether each pair of lines is parallel, intersecting or skew.
                (a) <m>DH</m> and <m>BF</m> (b) <m>AC</m> and <m>BD</m> (c) <m>EG</m> and <m>BC</m> (d) <m>HB</m> and <m>DF</m></p></statement>
            <answer><p>(a) parallel (b) intersecting, at the centre of the base (c) skew (d) intersecting, at the centre of the cuboid</p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-edges-skew-to-fg">
            <statement><p>List every edge of the cuboid <m>ABCDEFGH</m> that is skew to <m>FG</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>AB</m>, <m>AE</m>, <m>DC</m>, <m>DH</m></p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-tent-edges-skew-to-ac">
            <statement><p>For the tent <m>ABCDEF</m> in <xref ref="fig-tent-prism"/>, list every edge that is skew to <m>AC</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>BE</m>, <m>DE</m>, <m>EF</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>The tent has nine edges. <m>AB</m>, <m>AD</m>, <m>BC</m> and <m>CF</m> meet <m>AC</m> at <m>A</m> or <m>C</m>, and <m>DF</m> is parallel to <m>AC</m>. The other three, <m>BE</m>, <m>DE</m> and <m>EF</m>, never meet <m>AC</m> and are not parallel to it.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-name-the-projections">
            <statement><p>Name the projection of
                (a) <m>BH</m> on the base <m>ABCD</m> of the cuboid <m>ABCDEFGH</m>; (b) <m>AG</m> on the face <m>ADHE</m>; (c) <m>FD</m> on the face <m>DCGH</m>; (d) <m>VB</m> on the base of a right pyramid <m>VABCD</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p>(a) <m>BD</m> (b) <m>AH</m> (c) <m>DG</m> (d) <m>OB</m></p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-flagpole-and-wire">
            <statement><p>A flagpole <m>QR</m> stands vertically on level ground at a school in Nyeri, with <m>Q</m> at the top. A wire runs from <m>Q</m> to a peg <m>S</m> on the ground.
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Name the projection of the wire <m>QS</m> on the ground.</p></li>
                    <li><p>What is the projection of the pole itself?</p></li>
                    <li><p>A straight path on the ground does not pass through <m>S</m> or <m>R</m>. Is the wire skew to the path? Explain.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p></statement>
            <answer><p>(a) <m>RS</m> (b) the point <m>R</m> (c) yes</p></answer>
            <solution><p>(a) <m>S</m> is on the ground and <m>Q</m> is directly above <m>R</m>, so the projection is <m>RS</m>. (b) The pole is perpendicular to the ground, so it projects to its foot, the point <m>R</m>. (c) The wire meets the ground only at <m>S</m>, which is not on the path, so the wire and the path never meet. The wire rises while the path is level, so they are not parallel. They are skew.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-counting-skew-pairs">
            <title>Challenge: Counting Skew Pairs</title>
            <statement><p>How many pairs of edges of a cube are skew? How many pairs of edges of a square-based pyramid are skew?</p></statement>
            <hint><p>Count the edges skew to one edge, then share out, remembering that each pair is counted twice.</p></hint>
            <answer><p><m>24</m>; <m>8</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>Each of the <m>12</m> edges of a cube is skew to <m>4</m> others (as <m>AB</m> is skew to <m>CG</m>, <m>DH</m>, <m>FG</m> and <m>HE</m>). That counts each pair twice, so there are <m>12 \times 4 \div 2 = 24</m> skew pairs.</p>
            <p>In the pyramid <m>VABCD</m>, a base edge meets every other base edge or is parallel to it, and two sloping edges always meet at <m>V</m>. Each sloping edge is skew to the two base edges that do not touch its foot (for <m>VA</m>: <m>BC</m> and <m>CD</m>). So there are <m>4 \times 2 = 8</m> skew pairs.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <table xml:id="table-differentiation-skew-lines-and-projection">
            <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
            <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Work through activity parts (a) to (d) with the model in hand. Give a card: <q>Do they meet? Are they parallel? If neither, they are skew.</q> Work through <xref ref="example-parallel-intersecting-or-skew"/> only. Assign <xref ref="ex-classify-lines-in-a-cuboid"/> and <xref ref="ex-edges-skew-to-fg"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity plus all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-classify-lines-in-a-cuboid"/> to <xref ref="ex-flagpole-and-wire"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-counting-skew-pairs"/>. Extension: how many pairs of edges of a triangular prism are skew? (Answer: <m>12</m>. Each of the <m>6</m> edges of the two ends is skew to <m>3</m> edges and each of the <m>3</m> parallel edges is skew to <m>2</m>, so <m>(6 \times 3 + 3 \times 2) \div 2 = 12</m>.)</cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />

